## Incremental multi-platform organization

Keep current Host/Client fork locations and working Linux build paths initially.
Separate Linux and macOS capture, encoding, authentication, input, and session
implementations behind narrowly scoped interfaces only where both need them.
Keep protocol, transport, and portable session policy shared. Group packaging,
tests, and build instructions by platform as those components are introduced.
Do not perform a broad folder move or invent a general abstraction framework
before proving the macOS pipeline. Preserve fork history and re-run Linux
build/dependency gates whenever shared interfaces or paths change.

## Proposed architecture

    Ubuntu PLANK Client
        | existing authenticated control + native QUIC/RaptorQ
    macOS machine service
        | authenticated private IPC, session-scoped authorization
    active LoginWindow or Aqua graphical agent
        | ScreenCaptureKit -> shared pixel surfaces -> VideoToolbox
        | system audio capture -> existing audio transport
        | native keyboard / mouse input and cursor handling
        | optional normalized tablet input in a later phase

The diagram shows intended boundaries, not a proven process configuration.
Resolve exact process identities and transport ownership during the probes.

### Machine service and graphical agents

The bounded controller is now implemented as a standalone Python qualification
runner with native machine-state observation and signed graphical workers.
Build 31 passes a steady Aqua start/retire cycle; a build-30 injected worker
crash stops the controller and reclaims the display. Wrong UID/hash are refused.
The first real logout retired Aqua correctly but exposed early LoginWindow
identity readiness; build 32 adds a bounded initial wait without relaxing the
predicates. Independent inventory now runs in the active graphical domain,
because background root cannot reliably enumerate LoginWindow displays.
Build 32 passes a 180-second sign-in run with 1,706/1,706 encoded and no
overflow/drops, plus independently verified cleanup. A subsequent live
sign-in→4K-desktop run passed both lifecycle and strict media gates (538/538
and 266/266, no overflow/drops). A fresh reverse desktop→sign-in run also passed
lifecycle cleanup/replacement, including a 244 ms initial identity wait. It
failed the strict media gate with 22 desktop queue overflows; the sign-in
segment had none. Investigate intermittent 4K queue pressure before declaring
media qualification. Failure details are retained separately in the report.
Build 33 now has bounded, post-capture one-second timing summaries; its 1080p
sign-in baseline passes and the counters reconcile. It still needs operator
login for an idle 4K baseline before attributing the earlier overflows to
startup, normal capture or logout. No queue limit or handoff policy was changed.
Two subsequent idle desktop runs reproduced overflow at seconds 15 and 18,
without logout and outside the startup bucket. Encoder callbacks reached
~94 ms while submission/dispatch delays were much smaller. Controlled synthetic
color conversion and sparse submission comparisons remained ~14–16 ms and did
not reproduce the live delay. Next isolate SCK surface/timestamp interaction;
do not declare a root cause or change live color/queue policy from these tests.
Subsequent opt-in live comparisons found no improvement from retaining the
entire CMSampleBuffer or rebasing timestamps. Synthetic 1/60 PTS improved timing
but is not a valid streaming fix. Native speed-over-quality improved mean/p95
callback from ~30/47 ms to ~21/31 ms while preserving real timestamps; it remains
diagnostic-only. A low-latency encoder-specification request failed current HEVC
setup without fallback. Build 40 adds a bounded owned-chart compositor-readiness
check; baseline and speed moving-chart/decoded-color gates now both pass with
864/864 frames, no drops/overflow and nearly identical ~19 ms mean callbacks.
Independent bitstream inspection confirms exact 4K Main10/video-range metadata.
Before adopting speed priority, run longer mixed idle/moving tests and a
coordinated transition round trip. Default behavior remains intact; the small
marker chart does not qualify full-motion quality or sustained 4K60.
This is temporary launchd orchestration, not the eventual authenticated product
supervisor. Both transition directions now have lifecycle evidence, but no
zero-drop round trip or transport/input continuity qualification. Retain independent process/display cleanup checks,
first-encoded-frame readiness, exact geometry and separate lifecycle/media
results. The root operator designates the only test desktop UID; no remote
authentication or transport continuity is inferred from that scope.

Preparation now includes a bounded passive session observer and opt-in signed
session-guarded media probes (build 29). Validate the caller's Security session
graphical-access attribute, not only CG's reported console UID: an SSH process
can see the latter. Console/session-user notifications revoke the prior scope;
identity polling is a bounded backup, not authority to resume it. Synthetic
revocation and steady Aqua capture have cleanup passes; one revocation run had
a startup media overflow and remains a failed strict media result. Real
LoginWindow identification, cross-login agent replacement, notification timing,
lock/unlock and fast-user-switching safety remain unqualified. Next operator
step is logout to validate the strict LoginWindow predicates. Do not introduce
a persistent coordinator or claim continuous handoff from these preliminary
tests alone.

After operator logout, the unchanged build-29 strict predicates positively
identified LoginWindow. Its guarded 1080p HEVC test passed 168/168 frames,
zero drops/overflow and complete cleanup. Synthetic revocation passed 2/2
and normal removal. Thus both scopes have independent endpoint passes; next
verify the fresh desktop after operator login and implement/test coordinated
cross-session replacement. The fresh-desktop check is now complete with Screen
Sharing connected: actual 4K hardware HEVC, 45/45 encoded, no drops/overflow and
complete cleanup. Security-session ID remained the same across sign-in→Aqua;
UID/login phase must participate in identity checks. No media worker ran across
that transition, so continuous controller/agent replacement remains the next
implementation step. Do not equate separate probe launches with continuity.

Use launchd for persistent machine availability and session-specific agents
for GUI operations. The machine service coordinates authentication, exclusive
session ownership, and authorized attachment. Keep media processing outside
the privileged boundary wherever supported. Do not carry Linux's root media
worker design into macOS by default.

The current beta retains a mode-changed CGVirtualDisplay until its creator
process exits. A bounded standalone parent/child test verifies display removal
on normal exit and forced termination while the parent stays running. Therefore
qualify a small display-owner child under the graphical agent before product
integration. The isolated owner boundary now passes repeated tests in both Aqua
and LoginWindow. It would not be another persistent launchd service. Keep capture,
transport and authentication lifecycle separate: stop capture before retiring
the display owner, and verify removal before accepting a replacement layout.
The parent must independently validate geometry and child ownership; no public
unauthenticated display-control socket. Cross-session transitions remain untested.

Evaluate a graphical agent configured for LoginWindow and Aqua sessions using
the correct launchd session-type keys. A LaunchDaemon alone is not a graphical
session. Qualify capture and input separately in both contexts.

Use narrow, typed private IPC, preferably XPC, with peer identity/code-signing
verification and per-session authorization. A new agent cannot inherit access
to another account's desktop merely by connecting. Bound messages, queues,
timeouts, and descriptor/surface ownership. No shell-command proxy.

Prefer retaining the Client window and connection through agent replacement.
If transport continuity is not safely achievable initially, retain the window
and use bounded authenticated reconnect with accurate transition messages.
Revoke the old input path before enabling the replacement. Do not reuse a
stale display transform, pen contact, or account authorization.

### Authentication and permissions

Keep the PLANK authentication/session ownership contract, but implement account
verification through supported macOS facilities rather than copying Linux
PAM/SSSD assumptions. Define and review the exact API before integration.
Authenticating to PLANK does not automatically constitute logging into macOS;
qualify the normal LoginWindow workflow explicitly.

The first native backend now uses Open Directory `verifyPassword`, including
its documented policy evaluation, and matches UID/UUID to macOS membership
resolution. Twelve pure ownership cases and seven negative-input/root-denial
cases pass; real non-root development-account verification passes. This is a
short-lived local harness, not a desktop grant. Supervised private IPC now
passes 16 synthetic cases and real non-root account verification, with mutual
running-code checks, private inherited socket, four-second deadline and exact
child cleanup. Client-shaped conversation/token state passes 51 assertions for
ownership, peer binding, expiry, replay, revocation and bounded admission
(now 53 assertions with individual-token revocation). The native Network.framework
TLS 1.3 authentication adapter and live Aqua authority now pass loopback tests,
including the real account through the isolated helper. SSH is denied authority;
replay, malformed framing, old TLS, plaintext, untrusted certificate, stalled
requests and admission overflow fail closed. No existing-Client authentication
or stream is qualified yet. Public server information now passes native TLS and
the actual existing Client metadata parser on linux-client-builder (Qt 6.10.2), using a
shared XML fixture. It advertises authentication but explicitly no media or
topology capability yet; account/session/display data remain private. No Client
runtime changes were needed for this gate. Next implement authenticated topology
and explicit Apple capabilities, then claim authorization into native QUIC lifetime.
The Client now parses an explicit fixed-capture feature extension without
requiring the Mac to advertise Linux-specific layout/input/takeover features.
The original Linux feature mask, fixture and checks remain unchanged. Native
authenticated topology, the shared Apple profile descriptor and real Aqua
geometry tests pass. Bookmark creation/editing now includes the explicit Apple
profile, with append-only IDs and separately remembered bitrate; actual
serialization/reload tests pass without changing Linux profile values. A real
VideoToolbox-produced synthetic Main10 4:2:0 frame passes pinned Client FFmpeg
decode and strict limited-range BT.709/sRGB validation. Hardware decode and
renderer output are not yet qualified. Stream launch remains explicitly gated
off pending native authorization lifetime and optional audio/input/cursor
service negotiation; do not fake Linux capabilities. Public discovery retains zero media
readiness. The new bit must not enter the Linux launch feature mask. Also retain
the color distinction already measured: the probe's x420 input uses a BT.601
sample-matrix declaration, while VideoToolbox output is requested/qualified as
BT.709 primaries/matrix, sRGB transfer and video range. Input attachments are not
an output-bitstream contract; revalidate the integrated output and decoder.
See `docs/macos-control-plane.md` for the exact scope and lifecycle limits.
Reserved setup operation IDs are
not evidence that the current Client uses them for account authentication.
See `docs/macos-authentication.md` for limits and required policy tests.

Before exposing login-screen capture/input, require authenticated PLANK access.
After login, require active-desktop ownership. Retain explicit same-account
takeover and rejection of cross-account access. Test denied/disabled accounts,
wrong credentials, stale session credentials, and agent restart during login.

Identify Screen Recording, Accessibility/input, and any relevant entitlement
requirements on the exact target OS. Check whether persistent-content-capture
approval is applicable and available; do not assume signing or root bypasses
TCC. Document guided administrator provisioning and MDM options where supported.
If supported permission provisioning cannot meet unattended login-screen
access, stop and discuss instead of weakening the requirement silently.

### Capture, encoding, color, and audio

First establish a supported, capturable headless display in both LoginWindow
and Aqua. Inspect the current PCoIP display setup read-only and compare display
inventory with actual session geometry. Coordinate any PCoIP disconnect or
service-stop experiment with the user; do not remove the recovery path.
PLANK acceptance must ultimately prove operation without relying on a display
created or kept alive by PCoIP. If independent software display provisioning
requires unsupported APIs or a dongle, present the tradeoff before proceeding;
do not silently replace the headless requirement with a connected monitor.

Qualify useful resolution selection, logical-point/backing-pixel mapping,
mode persistence across reconnect/login/logout, and safe recovery after a
display agent exits. Continuous resizing can follow this stable baseline.

Feed ScreenCaptureKit frames into VideoToolbox using compatible shared pixel
surfaces. Prefer no CPU readback; measure actual copies and synchronization
rather than describing shared-buffer APIs as proof of zero-copy operation.
Use Metal for necessary scaling or RGB-to-YCbCr conversion, not an automatic
extra stage. Release surfaces promptly and bound in-flight frames.

Probe exact encoder capabilities, then encode and decode real test frames.
Request real-time hardware operation, disable frame reordering where supported,
and qualify live bitrate changes and keyframe requests. Hardware-labelled
profiles must fail clearly if hardware is unavailable, not silently use software.

Define explicit macOS profile tuples including capture precision, codec,
encoded bit depth/chroma, range, matrix, primaries, and transfer function.
Choose and validate a consistent SDR color contract; do not equate a 10-bit
surface with proven 10-bit capture or label YCbCr as RGB identity. Test range
endpoints, clipping, near-black values, ramps, saturated colors, and real footage.
ScreenCaptureKit tone mapping/color management must be understood and recorded.

Capture the authenticated desktop's system audio through ScreenCaptureKit if
qualified. Preserve current audio transport and Client OS-selected output.
Map audio/video timestamps to a common monotonic timeline, handle discontinuity
at session changes, and prevent audio from another user leaking across sessions.

### Client and protocol integration

Retain current PlankTransport/Kymux, KyProto/QUIC, RaptorQ, fixed-MTU negotiation,
congestion policy, input ordering, statistics, and per-bookmark bitrate behavior.
This is a platform port, not a network experiment or legacy protocol revival.

Add explicit per-bookmark Apple capture/VideoToolbox profiles only after probe
success. Advertise actual Host capabilities and reject unsupported tuples.
Do not reuse a Linux NVENC profile ID for an Apple encoder. Update shared
schemas, feature negotiation, vectors, and both Host/Client tests together.

The Ubuntu Client must validate exact-format hardware decoding first, then
exact-format software fallback where viable. Add/qualify correct 4:2:0 YCbCr
presentation; do not accidentally route these frames through the identity-GBR
presentation path. Existing Linux profiles and defaults remain unchanged.
Stats must identify actual capture, encoder, stream format, and presentation.

### Keyboard, mouse, and cursor first

Qualify absolute pointer position, buttons, drag, scrolling, modifiers, system
shortcuts, and custom cursor shapes/hotspots in ordinary desktop applications.
Preserve the Client's local toolbar and prevent duplicate captured/local
cursors. Test Retina scaling, unequal Host/Client resolution, and letterboxing.
Release held keys and buttons on failure, takeover, and session replacement.

### Later optional pen phase

First probe supported native tablet-event injection with correct event type,
proximity, position, tip-down/up, and pressure semantics. Event fields or a
successful API call alone do not prove an application consumes pressure.
Acceptance requires real pressure-varying strokes in an agreed macOS drawing
application, not Flame or a diagnostic pressure counter alone. Select that
application when this later phase begins.

Use the existing normalized pen protocol for macOS if suitable. Explicitly
negotiate it so the Client selects normalized events instead of a raw-HID-only
path; do not assume both streams are already sent. No tablet-model whitelist
or resolution-specific coordinate constants.

Retain Client mouse/local-toolbar behavior and support visible pen hover and
drawing with correct custom Host cursor shape/hotspot. Investigate native
cursor extraction independently: mouse rendering must not be taken as proof
that tablet cursor rendering works. Prevent a baked-in and local duplicate.

If native tablet injection cannot deliver reliable application pressure,
evaluate a narrowly scoped virtual HID alternative and its signing/entitlement
requirements. Pause for a decision before expanding into full device emulation.
Release pen contact, buttons, and keys on disconnect, takeover, agent failure,
and topology replacement. Test physical Host tablets coexisting with remote pen.

## Execution sequence and acceptance gates

Historical preview-first sequence (superseded by **Host first** above): finish the bounded mixed-cadence comparison, then move to the
first authenticated end-to-end preview instead of further isolated encoder
tuning. Qualify the unchanged native transport on the dedicated Mac using
`docs/macos-build-runbook.md`, then implement account/session authorization,
explicit Apple profile negotiation, complete-frame submission and basic input.
An already-logged-in desktop is an intermediate preview only; normal login-screen
access remains essential for the usable Host. Audio, Wacom and release packaging
do not block that preview. Several development days is a planning allowance,
not a delivery guarantee; login/session authorization and exact Client
presentation are still unimplemented integration gates.

The bounded comparison is now complete: actual idle-to-motion baseline has
602 overflows; speed priority has zero, with ~33.6 ms maximum callback and
passing initial color/normal cleanup. Its much higher chart bitrate use remains
a quality/efficiency tradeoff to assess with real content. The existing transport
also builds on macOS 27: 16 enabled unit tests and both native C ABI encrypted
loopbacks pass without runtime transport changes. Authentication, Apple profile
negotiation and interactive Client wiring are next. Details and build-only
toolchain/test-fixture corrections are in `docs/macos-build-runbook.md`.

1. Environment and permission feasibility.
   - Await the dedicated development Mac, verify Xcode readiness, and arrange Apple
     signing/provisioning. Pin the SDK/toolchain and dependencies.
   - Determine the existing headless display mechanism and establish a safe
     recovery procedure using retained PCoIP access. No tablet is required.
   - Confirm FileVault disabled without changing it; inventory permissions.
   - Deliver a documented capability/permission matrix. No product support
     claims from dormant upstream macOS files or SDK declarations alone.

2. Headless display, LoginWindow, and basic input probes.
   - Prove independent headless display availability and useful selectable
     resolutions; a desktop dependent on an active PCoIP display is not a pass.
   - Minimal machine service + graphical agent: capture changing frames and
     inject keyboard/mouse at the normal login screen and user desktop.
   - Boot without automatic login, remotely authenticate and log in, logout,
     reconnect, lock/unlock, and repeat. Reboots require operator coordination.
   - Prove unauthorized clients cannot view or control either context.
   - Test ordinary desktop navigation, scrolling, dragging, and cursor mapping.
   - Stop if headless display or login-screen access requires unsupported APIs or
     unresolved permissions. Preserve probe evidence; do not layer workarounds.

3. Apple media pipeline qualification.
   - ScreenCaptureKit -> shared buffers -> VideoToolbox real hardware encode.
   - Test HEVC 10-bit 4:2:0 and H.264 8-bit 4:2:0 at 2160p60 where supported.
   - Record capture format, actual encoder, color metadata, encode latency,
     frame ordering, copies, queue depth, bitrate response, and resource use.
   - Validate decoded images and prove any advertised 10-bit precision.

4. End-to-end PLANK integration.
   - Wire the proven native components to existing session/media contracts.
   - Add synchronized bookmark capabilities, profiles, stats, and exact decode.
   - Validate Client local toolbar, Native/Scaled-Span, mismatched resolutions,
     Retina logical-point/backing-pixel conversion, and full/windowed toggles.
   - Complete secure login/logout, reconnect, and explicit takeover transitions.

5. Performance, resilience, and Linux regression qualification.
   - Measure capture/encode/network/decode/render separately, including p50,
     p95, p99 and maximum stalls. Measure glass-to-glass and input-to-photon
     with an appropriate physical test; do not promise 8 ms from API choices.
   - Run static and moving content, scene cuts, text, scrolling, dragging, and audio.
     Require sustained requested frame rate with bounded queue depth/RSS.
   - Test Client-ingress random loss at 0.5/1/2/5/10%, burst loss, high bitrate,
     real outage, Wait/Disconnect, and recovery. Remove injected loss afterward.
   - Follow applicable Linux acceptance timing gates: 15-minute candidate run,
     two-hour A/V drift validation, eight-hour mixed-workload soak.
   - Requalify affected Linux profiles, Wacom pressure/Tablet Margins, cursor,
     toolbar, session ownership, transport, and package dependency gates.

6. Packaging and release qualification.
   - Add a dedicated macOS builder/path contract and reproducible clean-worktree
     runbook. Keep existing linux-host-builder/linux-client-builder roles unchanged.
   - Produce signed/notarized Host installation artifacts with launchd setup,
     guided permissions, dependency/license inventory, and exact provenance.
   - Choose/document native config, state, private TLS, and product-log paths;
     use local wall-clock timestamps and bounded retention. No credentials in
     repository, command lines, environment, or logs.
   - Test fresh install, upgrade, logout/reboot persistence, crash recovery,
     rollback, and clean uninstall without deleting administrator data.
   - Keep feature branch visible in candidate package/application versions.
     Merge/release only after user acceptance and the applicable gates pass.

7. Optional Wacom pressure enhancement, after general desktop acceptance.
   - Agree on a drawing test application and supply a Client tablet then.
   - Probe native normalized pen input before considering virtual HID.
   - Validate pressure, tip transitions, visible hover/drawing cursor, accurate
     positioning, reconnect, and cleanup. Tablet Margins are not required.
   - Do not block the general desktop release on this phase. Any shared Client
     changes must preserve existing Linux Wacom behavior and its test gates.

## Research references to revalidate against the selected SDK/OS

These projects are reference points, not implementations to copy verbatim.

- HiDPI mode ordering, persisted preferences, and display lifecycle reference
  (author-reported behavior must be reproduced on the development Mac):
  https://github.com/pasky/hidpi-mirror
- Concrete Objective-C++ virtual-display lifecycle reference; reviewed pitfalls
  and adaptation boundaries are recorded in macos-host-investigation.md:
  https://github.com/enfp-dev-studio/node-mac-virtual-display/blob/main/src/virtual_display.mm
- User-provided class-dump reference for the virtual-display API PCoIP imports
  (undocumented API, not an Apple public SDK header):
  https://github.com/w0lfschild/macOS_headers/blob/master/macOS/Frameworks/CoreGraphics/1336/CGVirtualDisplay.h
- Apple DTS LoginWindow/ScreenCaptureKit architecture discussion:
  https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/814152
- Persistent content capture entitlement and approval requirements:
  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/entitlements/com.apple.developer.persistent-content-capture
- ScreenCaptureKit color/capture configuration:
  https://developer.apple.com/videos/play/wwdc2024/10088/
- VideoToolbox capabilities and compression-session properties:
  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/videotoolbox
- Wacom native macOS tablet-event semantics:
  https://developer-docs.wacom.com/docs/icbt/macos/ns-events/ns-events-basics/
- Apple virtual HID alternative, only if native event tests fail:
  https://developer.apple.com/documentation/corehid/creatingvirtualdevices
- FileVault security implications:
  https://support.apple.com/guide/security/volume-encryption-with-filevault-sec4c6dc1b6e/web

These references guide experiments; none establishes that PLANK's macOS Host
already works. Record exact hardware/OS and actual results alongside each gate.

## Stopping point and resume checklist

**Current priority: Host first, as specified at the top of this plan.** Preserve
the Client checkpoints and stop preview-only integration. Next qualify system
audio on development-mac using the existing audio transport/decoder contract, then native
input/cursor and complete session lifecycle. No new Client build/deployment.

